--- Finding (latin_squares_in_reduced_form_randomizing_using_jacobson_and_matthews_technique):
--- a loop variable named like a Rust keyword (`move`, `loop`) is renamed out of
--- Rust's way only in the FIRST loop that binds it: a second `for` with the same
--- name, at the top level or inside one function, prints the bare keyword, and
--- the compiled program does not build.
---
--- crow run -i: a a b b
--- crow run:
---   error: expected identifier, found keyword `move`
---     --> src/main.rs:40:9
---   error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what
---   Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
--- `crow build --rs` shows `for move_ in 0_i64..(2_i64)` for the first loop and
--- `for move in 0_i64..(2_i64)` for the second. The same inside a function with
--- `for loop in range[n]` twice (expected identifier, found keyword `loop`).
--- One loop alone builds, as does a loop then an assignment of the same name.
--- Expected: a a b b in both modes (every binding of the name renamed).
for move in range[2]
    log['a']
for move in range[2]
    log['b']
